[Music]  so last week I got a chance to play some  Dragon Age Inquisition EA had a had a  little event here in the city and so it  was me and some other press members we  went in and it was basically just  unfettered time with the game starting  at the beginning and leading to as far  as we got in the in the three and a half  or four hours that we spent with it I  can't talk about everything due to  spoilers but I can certainly give you  some impressions of what I am allowed to  talk about and that starts with the  character creation because I'm was  pretty impressed with the character  creation unexpectedly so there are a lot  of ways of establishing a character you  get you get four races to choose from  human Alf canari and dwarf this is the  first time the Qunari has been playable  but I I ended up going with an elf and  I'm really happy that I did because I  got a chance to really play around to  character customization options in it  they're much more in depth than I ever  expected them to be so to give you an  example I mean you're used to being able  to set eye color and you know even in  certain games like the spacing of the  eyes and the eyebrows and all that kind  of stuff like how long the nose is how  big the joy is and it's got all that  kind of stuff but it goes even further  than that it's to the point where you  don't get to choose just I color but you  can choose the inner cornea color versus  the outer coordinate color to make eyes  look even more like real-life eyes and  there are tons of like tattoo options  and makeup options and all sorts of  stuff like that and so what I ended up  doing is I created this elf that I was  trying to make look very lovely and as I  went on I actually used some of the  makeup options and tattoo options and I  I gave them like really really cool eyes  with some white eyeliner and things like  that the downside is that by the time I  got into the actual game a character  that looked good in the character  creation screen I ended up sticking out  a little bit  the main game my poor elf looked a  little too colorful around his  compatriots but that would that was okay  another thing that I really liked about  it is that you get two you get several  different voice options even for the  same gender so if you choose a if you  choose a male character you have two  different male voices and if you choose  a female character II of two different  female voices I just hope I can help  everything you've heard completely true  so the rest of the stuff that I can talk  about is the gameplay in the hinterlands  which is this it really is a grassy  forest II area I was playing on the ps4  but I did get a chance to play around a  little bit with the with the tactical  camera which is not only back but isn't  just for PC players but also for console  players and and I didn't spend tons of  time with it but I but one thing that I  really liked is is the the forward  button so in other words in the past  like if you're playing I Swindell or  baldur's gate you go or Dragon Age even  and you go into the tactical camera you  know when you play those games you pause  issue your things unpause until the time  comes to pause again and then you pause  and in this case instead of pausing and  unpausing you have a forward button that  you just hold down to progress time  forward and let go when you when you  need to let it go and it's effectively  the same thing but it's it's a much  smoother kind of experience as a result  of that and I really like that they've  embraced that idea the rest of the time  though I kind of did it from a  third-person more action RPG kind of  perspective and actually that ended up  being a lot more satisfying than I  expected to be I played as a mage you  know you have your your main basic  attack which which you perform just by  holding that you know by holding a  shoulder button and then you hit you  know you hit various buttons to pull off  the the other spells in it it felt  really good there's a nice lock on that  you can use to lock on to enemies at  which point the camera sort of zooms in  so you get a better view of what's going  on otherwise there there's still Auto  targeting and when you when you perform  your spells but it'll go to whatever  enemy to get the game thinks is the  appropriate one whereas you can so  choose enemies by a walking console  Batman felt it felt right it felt very  good the hinterlands is also a big place  no I did it with a full party you get  your own character that you yourself  create you get Varrick who returns from  Dragon Age 2 as far as I know he's the  only returning character that can also  be a party number so that's fun or I  should say the only returning party  member that can now be a party member  but the Sandra also joins you in these  early hours and if you don't remember  who Cassandra is she's the she's the  Inquisitor that's interrogating Varrick  at the very beginning of Dragon Age 2 so  you get Cassandra with you and then you  get another mage in the end of the  exploration felt really good moving  around the hinterlands as was was really  easy and quests are really easy to take  so you know you've got your story quests  and the game leads you really well from  place to place with story quests but as  you go out into the world and this is  not like corridors going through I mean  it is somewhat like that but I think you  can think of it set up sort of like  Kingdoms of Amalur was where it's not an  entirely open world but they're large  open spaces that are connected either by  corridors or or by loading screens  there's not only quests that you come  across either by coming across people  that have stuff to offer you or by you  know wandering into say a Shack and  there's a diary there and then that can  initiate a quest for you as well I was  playing on regular difficulty there  wasn't at first I really didn't run into  any trouble and for the most part I  didn't I could play as sort of a  third-person action game with my people  at my side only occasionally pausing and  asking them to do things like use  potions and whatnot one time I did  stumble upon a dragon quite accidentally  and I initiated battle that involved not  just the large dragon flying overhead  and bombarding us but also little light  little live urns on the ground and I had  my ass handed to me pretty pretty easily  it would have been a good place to  actually start to micromanage that  battle but I had limited time so instead  of trying to bash against that  particular dragon I chose to go off and  do something else and come back later  you know what I played it seems like  they've got something that's a little  bit there for everybody depending on  what you like from RPGs I mean you know  it's it's easy to say hey this isn't as  much like Dragon Age Origins as I would  like but it does sort of have that and  it gives you an opportunity to play that  way if you want to I really like that  about it but also if you go into  something feeling like you want a little  bit more of an action RPG it feels  really good to play from just a typical  third-person perspective you also get to  level up your your skills and your  spell's and I didn't investigate that  tree too much I did level up my  characters as I went but the good news  is of course that you've still got  Varrick using Bianca beau and you will  get to upgrade that in certain ways but  the the trees looked a lot like they did  in Dragon Age 2 where in some cases you  get a chance to either you know when you  spell it you spend the skill point you  can spend it on something new or you can  spend it perhaps on an enhancement of  the one that you already have one  interesting thing about health potions  is that you start with eight total  potions across the party that you can  use and whenever you go back to camp  you replenish that they're not really  inventory items as it were I sort of  think of it more as the way Dark Souls  handles its Estus flasks which is like  you know this is the potions that you  get you get to refill the help anytime  you go back to a base camp so at the  stage it's clear to when you're when  you're making decisions and dialogue I  haven't done anything that's too wild  and crazy' just yet no great decision  when I was playing in the first three  hours but obviously ways to sort of  build up my character before that you  know do I want to be an [ __ ]  or you know do I want to be you know  nice about stuff and of course you get  to go back and forth between those if  you so desire and the game does tell you  how it affects your relationships with  other characters when you do that it  will it's sort of sort of Walking Dead  style in the sense that you know hey  your relationship has been improved  somewhat with this person or so-and-so  didn't like what you had to say so it's  a little bit more upfront then then  previous games were about how you're  affecting your relationships with other  people when you make those decisions  even though I only had like three and a  half hours four hours with the game or  so there's there's a lot more to talk  about and I could keep going on and on  but I think I'd probably get boring at  that point but suffice it to say I'm  actually really looking forward to  getting my hands on the full game at  this stage I don't know exactly what to  expect yet I mean I feel like Bioware is  in a position where they made people a  little bit unhappy with Dragon Age 2 not  being in the in the same ballpark as  Dragon Age 1 when it came to quality and  I feel like a lot of people got burn out  on that Mass Effect 3 ending so the  question is going to be is this is this  the the return to what people want from  Bioware and something tells me that you  know they're always going to be  naysayers there'll always be people that  that they complain about stuff but  something tells me that this this is a  good step towards getting people you  know back you know back on their side I  think that what I play the Dragon Age  Inquisition was was really good and  hopefully cool game ends up being just  as good as what I hope it would be  [Music]  [Music]

